<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/vectors-and-matrices">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>vector</strong> is a quantity with size and direction — an arrow you can add,
    scale and decompose. A <strong>matrix</strong> is a machine that transforms vectors: feed it
    a vector, it returns a new one, rotated, stretched or sheared. Together they are the
    mathematics of "anything with multiple dimensions at once" — graphics, robotics, quantum
    mechanics and machine learning all speak this language.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one vector, many uses — its components [x, y] are just a 2-entry matrix that matrices act on" widget="diagramBuilder" config={vecCfg} />

  <h2>Vector arithmetic you can feel</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Addition = walking:</strong> go right 5 up 3, then right 2 up 1 — total right 7 up 4. Components add separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Magnitude = Pythagoras:</strong> |v| = √(x² + y²); direction = atan2(y, x). The physics vector-diagram lesson visualises exactly this.</li>
    <li><strong>Dot product</strong> x₁x₂ + y₁y₂ measures alignment: zero means perpendicular. It powers search rankings and physics work calculations alike.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Matrices as transformations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A 2×2 matrix maps the whole plane:</strong> rotation matrices, scaling matrices, shears — each is four numbers with geometric meaning.</li>
    <li><strong>Determinant = area scale factor.</strong> det = 0 means the transformation crushes the plane onto a line — information lost, no inverse exists.</li>
    <li><strong>Eigenvectors</strong> are the directions a matrix doesn't rotate, only stretches — the hidden axes of any transformation, and the maths behind Google's original PageRank and PCA in data science.</li>
    <li><strong>Solving systems:</strong> Ax = b asks "which input vector produces this output?" — three equations, three unknowns, one matrix inverse.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This subject is the on-ramp to the expert rungs below: linear algebra done properly is the
    single most-used mathematics in modern applied work.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Strang, Introduction to Linear Algebra; 3Blue1Brown, Essence of Linear Algebra (visual intuition).</p>
</TopicPage>
